-- title:
--   Surjectivity of G(T) on Pic⁰ when ker G(T) is finite
-- statement:
--   Let $k$ be a finite field, $K$ an algebraically closed field, and $F_0$, $F$ fields with $F_0$ a $k$-algebra, $F$ both a $K$-algebra and an $F_0$-algebra, such that `IsCurveOver k F₀` and `IsCurveOver K F` hold: every nonzero element of the field has a divisor recording its orders at all places and of degree $0$, each place has residue field finite over the base field, and the module of Kähler differentials of the field over the base is free of rank $1$. Assume $F_0$ is generated over $k$ by a finite subset, and that $K$ together with the image of $F_0$ generates $F$, i.e. $\mathrm{adjoin}_K(\mathrm{im}(F_0\to F))=\top$. Let $\varphi\colon F\to F$ be a $K$-algebra endomorphism whose underlying ring homomorphism is integral and which satisfies $\varphi(x)=x^{q}$ on the image of $F_0$, where $q=\#k$. Let $T$ be an endomorphism of the additive group $\mathrm{Pic}^0(K,F)$ — the group of degree-zero divisors modulo the principal ones among them — such that $T$ sends the class of a degree-zero divisor $D$ to the class of its push-forward along $\varphi$ (place by place, restriction of places weighted by inertia degree). Let $G\in\mathbb{Z}[X]$ have constant coefficient nonzero in $K$, and suppose the kernel of $G(T)$, formed in the $\mathbb{Z}$-algebra of $\mathbb{Z}$-linear endomorphisms of $\mathrm{Pic}^0(K,F)$, is finite. Then $G(T)$ is surjective.
--
--   This is the function-field form of the statement that an endomorphism of the Jacobian with finite kernel is an isogeny, hence surjective on points over an algebraically closed field, applied to the ring generated by the Frobenius push-forward on $\mathrm{Pic}^0$. It is used in the computation of the order of $\ker G(T)$ as the absolute value of a resultant, in the counting of points on curves over finite fields.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicCurve_Pic0_surjective_aeval_of_finite_ker_of_pushforwardAlong_frobenius.lean

